  "full_response": "HTTPS, or Hypertext Transfer Protocol Secure, is the secure version of HTTP, the protocol over which data is sent between your browser and the website that you're connected to. HTTPS uses TLS, or Transport Layer Security, to protect the integrity and confidentiality of data between the user's computer and the site.\n\n\nThe process of using HTTPS and establishing a secure connection involves a handshake between the client's browser (user's computer) and the server. Here's a simplified version of the TLS handshake process:\n\n\n1. The client requests a secure connection to the server and the web browser sends a list of supported ciphers to the server.\n\n2. The server selects a cipher and sends its public key along with its digital certificate back to the client. The digital certificate has been issued by a trusted certificate authority (CA) and proves the server is who it claims to be.\n\n3. The client verifies the server's certificate with the CA to ensure it hasn't been revoked. It's also where the client checks the intended server's hostname against the certificate's domain name to prevent man-in-the-middle attacks.\n\n4. Once the client confirms the certificate is valid and hasn't been revoked, it creates a pre-master secret, encrypts it with the server's public key, and sends it to the server.\n\n5. On the server side, the pre-master secret is decrypted with the server's private key, and both the client and server independently compute the session keys from the pre-master secret.\n\n6. After these keys are generated, both client and server can reconfirm their identities, and they exchange encrypted messages using these session keys for the duration of the connection.\n\n\nBy the end of the handshake, both parties have loaded the session keys, checked each other's authenticity, and established an encrypted channel. This process ensures that all the following communication between both parties cannot be intercepted and read by an unauthorized party. The remaining part of the communication happens within this encrypted channel, by which HTTPS means all transmitted data (like your password, credit card numbers, or personal information) are protected against eavesdropping and tampering.",
